Loaded playbook: blazor_wasm
Description: Assess a .NET application that ships assemblies to the client (Blazor WebAssembly and similar) for exposed secrets, and for server endpoints that trust client-derived material. The client download is unauthenticated: treat every assembly the browser receives as public, readable source. Decompile before analysing — searching raw binaries is not a substitute.
Mode: agent

╭───────────────────────────────────────────── Starting ─────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                                │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                                      │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                                    │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                                        │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                                     │
│ Max loops: 60                                                                                      │
│ Task: Assess a .NET application that ships assemblies to the client (Blazor WebAssembly and        │
│ similar) for exposed secrets, and for server endpoints that trust client-derived material. The     │
│ client download is unauthenticated: treat every assembly the browser receives as public, readable  │
│ source. Decompile before analysing — searching raw binaries is not a substitute.                   │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Fingerprint the client-side .NET delivery                                                   │
│ Objective: Decide whether .NET assemblies are shipped to the client, and find the manifest or      │
│ loader that lists them. Filenames and layout vary by .NET version and by product — investigate     │
│ what is actually served rather than assuming a fixed path.                                         │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Read the site root and app host pages and look at how the app boots. A loader referencing     │
│ WebAssembly means assemblies ship; a server-side render mode means they do not and this playbook   │
│ does not apply. Newer unified loaders can mean either - determine which rather than guessing.      │
│   2. Asset names are frequently fingerprinted with a content hash, so match on stems and patterns, │
│ not exact filenames.                                                                               │
│   3. Respect any <base href> - the framework directory is relative to it, so probe both the site   │
│ root and the base path.                                                                            │
│   4. Find the assembly inventory by whatever route exists: a boot manifest file, a boot config     │
│ embedded in or fetched by the runtime JavaScript, or the loader script itself. If one route does   │
│ not exist on this target, try the next; the loader must reference the assets somehow.              │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Prepare the decompilation toolchain                                                         │
│ Objective: Get a working IL decompiler BEFORE analysing anything, then stop thinking about         │
│ tooling. Skipping this produces false negatives; over-investing in it wastes the entire step       │
│ budget.                                                                                            │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Check what is already available in ONE compound command rather than a sequence of separate    │
│ probes: look for common decompilers on PATH, in per-user tool directories, and for a .NET runtime. │
│ Batch independent checks - you may also issue several tool calls in a single turn.                 │
│   2. If a decompiler is present, immediately prove it actually runs (invoke its help or version).  │
│ If it works, the toolchain is DONE: stop, record it, and move to the next phase. Do not install    │
│ anything and do not evaluate alternatives. Toolchain hunting is the single largest time sink in    │
│ this assessment.                                                                                   │
│   3. Record the exact working invocation - including any environment prefix - both as a note and   │
│ to a file on disk. THIS PHASE IS ONCE-ONLY: before any later tooling check or install, read that   │
│ back and reuse it verbatim. A decompiler proven earlier in the session stays valid even if you no  │
│ longer recall proving it.                                                                          │
│   4. A .NET decompiler distributed as a managed tool needs a matching runtime. If it is found but  │
│ refuses to launch complaining the runtime is missing, the runtime is usually present but           │
│ undiscovered - set the appropriate environment variable to point at it and retry once before       │
│ concluding it is unavailable.                                                                      │
│   5. Only if nothing usable exists, install by the least invasive route available: an existing     │
│ SDK's tool installer, the vendor's rootless install script, a distro package if the repo carries   │
│ one, or a container image that already has the SDK. Stop at the first success.                     │
│   6. NEVER GUESS A VERSION NUMBER. If an install fails, the usual cause is that the newest         │
│ published release targets a newer runtime than the SDK installed here - so pinning an older        │
│ release is right, but you must look up which releases exist instead of inventing one. Query the    │
│ package feed for the actual version list, then choose from it. Version schemes vary and are not    │
│ always three-part: a package may publish four-part versions, so a plausible-looking guess will     │
│ simply not exist and every attempt will fail identically no matter how many you try.               │
│   7. Check what runtime you actually have before choosing that version, and pick a release built   │
│ for it rather than the newest one. Two failed installs of the same package mean your version       │
│ string is wrong, not that the package is unavailable.                                              │
│   8. HARD LIMIT: at most SIX install attempts across ALL routes for the entire assessment. Count   │
│ them. On the sixth failure stop trying to install anything and go to the fallback below. A seventh │
│ attempt has never once succeeded and the budget spent here is taken directly from finding          │
│ vulnerabilities.                                                                                   │
│   9. Two routes are forbidden because they cannot work and have wasted entire runs: (1) Python and │
│ JavaScript package managers do not carry a .NET decompiler - `pip install` of anything             │
│ decompiler-shaped is always wrong, whatever the name looks like; (2) do not clone or build a       │
│ decompiler from source. If you have typed either, stop and go to the fallback.                     │
│   10. REQUIRED FALLBACK, and it usually works: if no decompiler can be obtained, extract string    │
│ literals directly from the assemblies as UTF-16. .NET stores literals in the #US metadata heap     │
│ encoded UTF-16LE, so a default ASCII strings pass shows almost nothing and looks like the file     │
│ holds no secrets - which is exactly the false negative that makes this step feel pointless. Use    │
│ the little-endian 16-bit encoding option of your strings utility (on GNU binutils, the `-e l`      │
│ flag). This is a last resort, not a substitute for decompiling: it recovers literal values but not │
│ the code that uses them, so say in your findings that you could not decompile and note what that   │
│ leaves unproven.                                                                                   │
│   11. An IL disassembler is also an acceptable fallback if a C# decompiler cannot be obtained; say │
│ so in your findings if you had to fall back.                                                       │
│   12. Time-box this. Working decompiler: move on. Six failed install attempts: use the fallback,   │
│ note the limitation, and CONTINUE THE ASSESSMENT - failing to obtain a tool is never a reason to   │
│ end the run without analysing the assemblies you already hold.                                     │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Retrieve the shipped assemblies                                                             │
│ Objective: Download what the browser actually receives.                                            │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Read manifests with a real GET. A HEAD request returns headers only and tells you nothing     │
│ about contents.                                                                                    │
│   2. Prioritise application assemblies over framework/runtime ones. Shared libraries referenced by │
│ both client and server are the highest-value targets, because developers often forget that half of │
│ a shared project ships to the browser.                                                             │
│   3. Verify each download is a real assembly and not an error page or empty file - check size and  │
│ leading bytes. Keep everything in ONE working directory and do not re-download assemblies you      │
│ already have; repeated downloads into new directories waste the budget.                            │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Decompile to source before searching                                                        │
│ Objective: Convert retrieved assemblies to readable source. Do this first - analysis of raw        │
│ binaries is unreliable.                                                                            │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Decompile each assembly as the NEXT action after retrieving it. Do not search, grep or triage │
│ the binaries first.                                                                                │
│   2. Know whether your invocation produced a directory of sources or a single file, and remember   │
│ which - later searching a path as if it were the other form silently returns nothing and looks     │
│ like the decompile failed.                                                                         │
│   3. Decompile EVERY application assembly retrieved, not just the first or the most obviously      │
│ named. The entry-point client assembly often holds only UI code while the interesting material     │
│ sits in a referenced shared library.                                                               │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Hunt secrets in the decompiled source                                                       │
│ Objective: Find embedded credentials and sensitive material. Search along several INDEPENDENT axes │
│ and iterate — no single pattern is sufficient, and you are expected to devise your own.            │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Search the decompiled SOURCE, never the binaries, and always with line numbers so you can     │
│ navigate to hits.                                                                                  │
│   2. Cover these independent axes, designing patterns yourself and refining them as you learn what │
│ this codebase looks like:                                                                          │
│   - BY NAME: identifiers and comments suggesting credentials.                                      │
│   - BY DECLARATION: compile-time constants and static readonly fields, which is where embedded     │
│ values structurally live.                                                                          │
│   - BY SHAPE: string literals that look like key material - long, unbroken, high-entropy,          │
│ base64/hex-like, or padded/repeating.                                                              │
│   - BY USE: cryptographic and auth APIs, and what gets passed to them.                             │
│   - BY CONFIG: endpoints, hostnames and connection strings.                                        │
│   3. TRACE INDIRECTION. A crypto call usually references an identifier rather than a literal. That │
│ is a lead, not an answer: search for that identifier's definition and repeat until you reach the   │
│ actual value. Expect several hops, and report the value, not the variable name.                    │
│   4. Do not read whole decompiled files looking for secrets - they run to thousands of lines and   │
│ output is truncated long before the interesting line. Search for candidates, then read only the    │
│ surrounding lines of each hit.                                                                     │
│   5. Record each finding with the assembly, namespace, type and member it came from, and note      │
│ whether that assembly is referenced by the client directly or transitively - a secret in a shared  │
│ library usually means nobody realised it shipped at all.                                           │
│                                                                                                    │
│ Phase: Exploit server trust in client material                                                     │
│ Objective: Determine what the recovered material unlocks. A key is only a finding once you show    │
│ what it grants.                                                                                    │
│ Techniques:                                                                                        │
│   1. Enumerate the API surface before choosing a target - the endpoint that trusts this material   │
│ may not be the obvious one, and may appear in API metadata or a schema document you already have.  │
│   2. Reimplement the client's routine from the decompiled source exactly; the client code is the   │
│ specification.                                                                                     │
│   3. Exercise the endpoint with attacker-controlled input protected by the recovered material, and │
│ compare against what an unauthenticated caller gets without it.                                    │
│   4. Inspect responses for data the public API does not otherwise expose - credentials, tokens,    │
│ internal identifiers, or fields absent from the documented contract.                               │
│                                                                                                    │
│                                                                                                    │
